"SB 502 (2022) Mandated Framework Regulations Amendments | Department of Toxic Substances Control (https://dtsc.ca.gov/sb-502-2022-mandated-framework-regulations-amendments/)\nciteturn1219search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: last week; # SB 502 (2022) Mandated Framework Regulations Amendments  * DTSC Reference Number(s): R-2023-15R ...   * OAL Regulatory Action Number(s): 2026-0417-04S ...   * Final Regulatory Text\n\n## Regulatory Review Unit\n\nThe Director’s Office – Regulatory Review Unit (RRU) provides expert review of proposed regulations and guides DTSC programs through the Administrative Procedure Act to produce legally sound, transparent, and clear regulations that protect public health and the environment.\n\n# SB 502 (2022) Mandated Framework Regulations Amendments\n\n  * DTSC Reference Number(s): R-2023-15R\n  * OAL Notice File Number(s): Z-2025-0527-03\n  * OAL Regulatory Action Number(s): 2026-0417-04S\n\n## Regulation Approved\n\nOAL Approval Date: 06/01/2026\n\nSecretary of State Filing Date: 06/01/2026\n\nEffective Date: 10/01/2026\n\nThe following documents are part of the official rulemaking file:\n\n  * Notice of Approval\n  * Final Regulatory Text\n  * Final Statement of Reasons\n\n## 45-Day Notice of Proposed Action\n\nComment Period: 06/06/2025 – 07/21/2025\n\n  * Notice of Proposed Action (NOPA)\n  * Proposed Regulatory Text\n  * Initial Statement of Reasons (ISOR)\n  * Economic and Fiscal Impact Statement (Form STD 399)\n  * Form STD 400\n\nTo submit any comment(s) for this rulemaking file during the open comment period, please visit the CalSAFER webpage.\n\nShould you have any questions or concerns, feel free to send an email to the project manager for this rulemaking file, Cathy Valentine, at [email protected]. If Valentine is unavailable, you may contact the Office of Legislation and Regulatory Review at [email protected].--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicroplastics in Consumer Products | Department of Toxic Substances Control (https://intl.dtsc.ca.gov/scp/microplastics-in-consumer-products/)\nciteturn1219search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Building on California’s leadership to reduce plastic pollution, DTSC’s Safer Consumer Products (SCP) Program is researching microplastics in consumer products to prevent or reduce the release of microplastics throughout the product lifecycle. ... June 2026  | DTSC added microplastics to the Candidate Chemicals List, effective October 1, 2026.\n\n## Safer Consumer Products\n\nWe are working toward safer California households, workplaces, and products.\n\n# Microplastics in Consumer Products\n\nImage: Image of a hand full of microplastics.Plastic pollution is a global challenge with serious impacts on California’s environment, wildlife, and potentially human health. Each year, millions of tons of plastics enter the world’s oceans, and California communities are estimated to spend more than $428 million annually to manage plastic waste. Plastics don’t fully degrade—they fragment into microplastics, which persist in ecosystems and food chains.\n\nBuilding on California’s leadership to reduce plastic pollution, DTSC’s Safer Consumer Products (SCP) Program is researching microplastics in consumer products to prevent or reduce the release of microplastics throughout the product lifecycle.\n\n## DTSC is conducting preliminary research on products that release or generate microplastics.\n\nWe have added microplastics to our Candidate Chemical List and are conducting preliminary research. At this time, DTSC has not proposed to regulate any Priority Products containing or generating microplastics.\n\n## DTSC’s Definition of Microplastics\n\nMicroplastics are plastics that are less than 5 millimeters (mm) in their longest dimension. They can be classified as:\n\n  * Primary microplastics that are manufactured and intentionally added to products, such as microbeads in personal care products; or\n  * Secondary microplastics that arise from the degradation of plastic products, such as fragments from degradation of plastic bags or water bottles.\n\nTo learn more about our work on adding microplastics to the Candidate Chemicals List, please visit the Microplastics Candidate Chemicals listing webpage.\n\n## Rationale for Researching Microplastics\n\nMicroplastics are persistent, mobile, and widespread in the environment. They have been detected in every ecosystem, including water, air, soil, drinking water, and foods and beverages that humans consume.\n\nHumans and animals are exposed to microplastics on a regular basis via ingestion, inhalation, and dermal routes. Microplastics have been detected in human blood, lungs, placentas, testes, breast milk, and stool samples. Wildlife can mistake microplastics for food, which can result in survival and reproductive impairment. Exposure to microplastics may cause physical stress and damage, disrupt endocrine and immune systems, and negatively impact mobility, reproduction, and development.\n\n## Background on DTSC’s Microplastics Research Approach\n\nWe began screening microplastics in consumer products based on our 2024–2026 Priority Product Work Plan, which prioritizes reducing microplastics release into the environment from consumer products across their life cycle and includes products that contain or generate microplastics.\n\nWe initially evaluated a broad list of consumer product categories, including synthetic textiles, motor vehicle parts, beauty and personal care products, children’s toys, artificial turf, building materials, and cleaning products. A detailed list of products is available in Appendix A of the Background Document on Microplastics in Consumer Products.     * Background Document\n    * Workshop Recording\n    * Workshop Presentation Slides\n    * Workshop Agenda\n    * Public Comments\n\n## History and Chronology\n\nDate  | Description\n--- | ---\nJune 2026  | DTSC added microplastics to the Candidate Chemicals List, effective October 1, 2026.\nJanuary 2026  | DTSC received public comments on the Background document on Microplastics in Consumer Products Research. Review submitted comments at CalSAFER.\nDecember 2025  | DTSC hosted a public workshop on Background document on Microplastics in Consumer Products Research. Materials from that workshop are available on our 2025 Workshops and Events Webpage. Listen to our workshop recording at:\nDTSC Microplastics in Consumer Products Research Public Workshop - December 11, 2025.\nNovember 2025  | DTSC published a public background document summarizing our preliminary research on products that contain or generate microplastics.\nJune 2025  | DTSC initiated a rulemaking to add microplastics to the Candidate Chemicals List. For more information on the basis for this action, please refer to the June 2025 Microplastics Technical Document.\nJune 2023  | DTSC seeks public comment on a proposal to add microplastics to the Candidate Chemicals List. DTSC convenes a workshop on June 27, 2023, to discuss this proposal and to get feedback from the public. For more information on the basis for this proposal, please refer to the 2023 Microplastics Background Document or Microplastics Candidate Chemicals Proposed Listing CalSAFER web page.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\nQ. Why is DTSC researching consumer products that may contain or generate microplastics?\nA. In the 2024–2026 Priority Product Work Plan, DTSC identified policy considerations to inform our evaluation of the outlined product categories. These policy considerations included reducing the release of microplastics into the environment during all stages of the consumer product life cycle. With this policy consideration in mind, we added “products that contain or generate microplastics” as a product category in the Work Plan and began researching microplastics in consumer products and their impacts on human health, wildlife, and the environment.\n\nQ. Has DTSC prioritized products for regulation?\nA. No. Our preliminary screening research identified consumer products that contain microplastics or generate microplastics during the product lifecycle. At this time, we are seeking input on our initial research.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2026 Regular Session (https://www.legis.la.gov/Legis/ViewDocument.aspx?d=1470351)\nciteturn1219search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; 2026 Regular Session ... SBS FINAL PASSAGE ... SB 502 BY CATHEY\n2026 Regular Session\nSequence: 1023\nSBS FINAL PASSAGE\n Date: 5/07/2026\nTime: 3:24:40 PM\nSB 502 BY CATHEY\nFINAL PASSAGE\nROLL CALL\nThe roll was called with the following result:\nYEAS\nMr. Speaker\nAdams\nAmedee\nBacala\nBamburg\nBayham\nBeaullieu\nBerault\nBillings\nBoudreaux\nBourriaque\nBoyd\nBrass\nBraud\nBroussard\nButler\nCarpenter\nCarrier\nCarter, R.\nCarver\nChassion\nChenevert\nCoates\nCox\nCrews\nDeshotel\nDewitt\nDickerson\nDomangue\nEchols\nEgan\nFarnum\nFirment\nFisher\nFontenot\nFreiberg\nGadberry\nGalle\nGeymann\nGlorioso\nHebert\nHenry, C.\nHilferty\nHorton\nIllg\nJackson\nJohnson, M.\nJohnson, T.\nJordan\nKerner\nKnox\nLaCombe\nLaFleur\nLandry, J.\nLandry, M.\nLarvadain\nLyons\nMack\nMartinez\nMcFarland\nMcMahen\nMcMakin\nMelerine\nMuscarello\nOrgeron\nOwen\nPhelps\nRiser\nSawyer\nSchamerhorn\nSchlegel\nSpell\nSt. Blanc\nStagni\nTarver\nThomas\nThompson\nTurner\nVillio\nWilder\nWright\nWyble\nYoung\nZeringue\n Total -- 84\nNewell\nTaylor\nNAYS\nBryant\nFreeman\nHenry, D.\nMarcelle\nMena\nMiller\nMoore\nMurray\n Total -- 10\nVentrella\nWalters\nWiley\nABSENT\nBagley\nBoyer\nCarlson\nCarter, W.\nEdmonston\nGreen\nLandry, T.\nMcCormick\n Total -- 11--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2026 Regular Session (https://www.legis.la.gov/legis/ViewDocument.aspx?d=1470351)\nciteturn1219search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; 2026 Regular Session ... SBS FIN